.\"	$GergiOS: macctl.8,v 1.0 2026/07/10 $
.\"
.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt MACCTL 8
.Os GergiOS
.Sh NAME
.Nm macctl
.Nd control MAC enforcement at runtime
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm on
.Nm
.Cm off
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility controls the MAC (Mandatory Access Control) policy daemon
.Xr macd 8 .
It allows the administrator to enable or disable MAC enforcement
at runtime without restarting the daemon.
.Pp
When enforcement is disabled,
.Xr macd 8
returns
.Dv MAC_ALLOW
for all policy checks, effectively allowing all operations.
The policy rules are still loaded and can be inspected, but they
are not enforced.
.Pp
The commands are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm status
Show whether MAC enforcement is currently enabled or disabled,
and how many policy rules are loaded.
.It Cm on
Enable MAC enforcement.
All subsequent MAC policy checks will be evaluated against the
loaded rules.
.It Cm off
Disable MAC enforcement.
All subsequent MAC policy checks will return
.Dv MAC_ALLOW
without consulting the policy engine.
.It Fl h
Show usage summary.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/macd.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/macd.conf
Text-format MAC policy file.
.It Pa /etc/macd.policy
Binary-format MAC policy file (compiled by mac-compile).
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check whether MAC is currently enforcing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
macctl status
.Ed
.Pp
Disable MAC enforcement temporarily (e.g., for debugging):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
macctl off
.Ed
.Pp
Re-enable MAC enforcement:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
macctl on
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr macd 8 ,
.Xr mac-compile 8 ,
.Xr macd.conf 5
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
command first appeared in GergiOS 1.0.
